          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong homotopy pullback
            is a special kind of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            in the context of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+in+quasi-categories"} quasi-category pullbacks
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
            that is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} presented
            by a given
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
            = succeed "." do
              %strong homotopy fiber products
          %p
            The notion of
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushout"} homotopy pushout
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dual
            concept.
          %p
            For more details see
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            a homotopy pullback is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpretation
            of the space of solutions to an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation"} equation
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#in_category_theory In category theory
          %p
            As with all homotopy limits, there is both a
            %em local
            and a
            %em global
            notion of homotopy pullback.
          %p
            The
            %em global
            definition says that the homotopy pullback of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-span"} cospan
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ←
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Z \leftarrow Y
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is its image under the right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi pb
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mo →
                        %mo ←
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pb: C^{\to \leftarrow} \to C
          %p
            The
            %em local
            definition says that the homotopy pullback of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ←
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Z \leftarrow Y
            in a category with a notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            consists of a square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  P &amp; \to&amp; Y
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  X &amp;\to&amp; Z
                  }
          %p
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+square"} commutes
            up to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi T
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  T &amp; \to&amp; Y
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  X &amp;\to&amp; Z
                  }
          %p
            that commutes up to homotopy, there exists a morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\to P
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi HoSq
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ←
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map(T,P) \simeq HoSq(T,X\to Z\leftarrow Y)
          %p
            between the space of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\to P
            and the space of homotopy commutative squares with vertex
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            In good situations, such as when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y,Z
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+object"} fibrant
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
          %p
            Note that in both cases, there is a canonical map from the actual pullback
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Z
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times_Z Y
            to the homotopy pullback
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Z
                      %mi h
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times_Z^h Y
          %h3#InHomotopyTypeTheory In homotopy type theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            the homotopy pullback of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f : A \to C
          %p along a term of function type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  g : B \to C
          %p
            is given formally by precisely the same formula that would also define the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
            of functions of sets, namely by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left\{
                  a : A, b : B \;\; | \;\; f(a) = g(b)
                  \\right\}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Spelled out, this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mrow
                              %mi a
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                              %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mrow
                              %mi b
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                              %mi B
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underset{
                  \\array{
                  {a \colon A}
                  \\\
                  {b\colon B}
                  }
                  }{\sum}
                  \\big(
                  f(a) = g(b)
                  \\big)
          %p
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation"} evaluation
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
          %p
            What in classical logic is interpreted as the set of pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b)
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(b)
            are equal here becomes the restriction of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cocylinder"} mapping cocylinder
          %p
            Formal proof that this is the homotopy pullback in homotopy type theory is in (
            %a{:href => "#Brunerie"}> Brunerie
            ). Proof in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics+of+homotopy+type+theory"} categorical semantics of homotopy type theory
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ConstructionInHomotopyTypeTheory"} below
          %h2#ConcreteConstructions Concrete constructions
          %p
            We discuss various concrete constructions by ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
            and ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            such that under some sufficient conditions these compute homotopy pullbacks, up to weak equivalence.
          %h3#ConstructionsGeneral General
          #HomotopyPullbackByOrdinaryPullback.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mo ←
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to C \leftarrow B
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
              in some
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times_C B
              to present the homotopy pullback of the diagram are
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  one of the two morphisms is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration"} fibration
                  and all three objects are
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+objects"} fibrant objects
              %li
                %p
                  one of the two morphisms is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration"} fibration
                  and the model category is
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+proper+model+category"} right proper
          %p
            Both statements are classical. They are reviewed for instance as
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Lurie"} Lurie, prop. A.2.4.4
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "proper+model+category#HomotopyLimits"} here
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Notice that a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+resolution"} fibrant resolution
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
              in the injective
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+functors"} model structure on functors
              has
              %em both
              morphisms be a fibration. So the first point in Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomotopyPullbackByOrdinaryPullback"}
              says that (in the special case of pullbacks) something weaker than fibrancy is sufficient for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} deriving
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
              functor on cospan diagrams.
            %p
              This can be explained in model-categorical terms by the fact that the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospans"} cospans
              also has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+model+structure"} Reedy model structure
              in which the fibrant objects are precisely those considered in the first point in Prop.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomotopyPullbackByOrdinaryPullback"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Const
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Lim
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Const \dashv Lim
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} Quillen
              with respect to it).
            %p
              In this spirit one may ask for the largest class of morphisms such that their ordinary pullbacks are already homotopy pullbacks. This is related to the concept of
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+morphisms"} sharp morphisms
          #NeedForThreeFibrantObjects.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              In the first point of Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomotopyPullbackByOrdinaryPullback"}
              it is indeed necessary that all three objects are fibrant. As a (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counterexample"}> counter-
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/example"} example
              consider the pullback of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda_1^2 \to \Delta^2
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_1 \colon \Delta^1 \to \Delta^2
              in
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SimplicialSets"} SimplicialSets
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joyal+model+structure"} Joyal model structure
          %p
            Due to prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomotopyPullbackByOrdinaryPullback"}
            one typically computes homotopy pullbacks of a diagram by first forming a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
            of one of the two morphisms by a fibration and then forming an ordinary pullback.
          #HomotopyPullbackByFactorizationLemma.num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              If in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi C
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi g
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \stackrel{f}{\to} C \stackrel{g}{\leftarrow} B
              all three objects are
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+objects"} fibrant objects
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                            %msubsup
                              %mo ×
                              %mi C
                              %mi h
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mi I
                            %msub
                              %mo ×
                              %mi C
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A\times_C^h B &amp; \longrightarrow &amp; C^I \times_C B
                    \\\
                    \\big\downarrow &amp;&amp; \big\downarrow
                    \\\
                    A &amp; \underset{f}{\longrightarrow} &amp; C
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              or, equivalently up to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            #eq:HomotopyPullbackAsFiberProductWithPaths.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                            %msubsup
                              %mo ×
                              %mi C
                              %mi h
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mi I
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                            %mo ×
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi f
                                %mo ,
                                %mi g
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                            %mo ×
                            %mi C
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A\times_C^h B
                    \&amp; \longrightarrow &amp;
                    C^I
                    \\\
                    \\big\downarrow &amp;&amp; \big\downarrow
                    \\\
                    A\times B
                    \&amp;
                    \\underset{(f,g)}{\longrightarrow}
                    \&amp;
                    C\times C
                    }
                    \\,.
          %p
            See also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mayer-Vietoris+sequence"} Mayer-Vietoris sequence
            (
            %a{:href => "Mayer-Vietoris+sequence#SequenceFromDiagonal"}> this proposition
            ) and at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equalizer"} homotopy equalizer
            (
            %a{:href => "homotopy+equalizer#RelationToHomotopyPullbacks"}> this section
            ).
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Since the objects are already fibrant, prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomotopyPullbackByOrdinaryPullback"}
              implies that it is sufficient to replace one of the morphisms by a fibrant resolution. Such a resolution is provided by the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+lemma"} factorization lemma
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "factorization+lemma#FibrantResolution"} Lemma 3
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to C
              admits a canonical fibrant resolution
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                    %mi B
                    %mo ↠
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^I \times_C B \twoheadrightarrow C
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} C^I \to C \times C
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space+object"} path space object
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              (for instance, when
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+homotopical+category"} closed monoidal homotopical category
              then this can be taken to be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
              with an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
          #HomotopyFiberSequenceOfHomotopyPullback.num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              Incidentally, when
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,C
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \longrightarrow C
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \longrightarrow C
              are pointed maps, then
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:HomotopyPullbackAsFiberProductWithPaths"} (1)
              implies that the homotopy fiber product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                      %mi h
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times_C^h B
              sits in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+sequence"} homotopy fiber sequence
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi C
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi A
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                      %mi h
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Omega C
                    \\longrightarrow
                    A \times^h_C B
                    \\longrightarrow
                    A \times B
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is because the following outer rectangle is a (homotopy) pullback exhibiting the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/based+loop+space"} based loop space
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Ω
                            %mi C
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                            %msubsup
                              %mo ×
                              %mi C
                              %mi h
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mi I
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                            %mo ×
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi f
                                %mo ,
                                %mi g
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                            %mo ×
                            %mi C
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\Omega C
                    \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                    A\times_C^h B
                    \&amp; \longrightarrow &amp;
                    C^I
                    \\\
                    \\big\downarrow
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\big\downarrow
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\big\downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\ast
                    \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                    A\times B
                    \&amp;
                    \\underset{(f,g)}{\longrightarrow}
                    \&amp;
                    C\times C
                    \\mathrlap{\,,}
                    }
            %p
              which implies the claim by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+law"} pasting law
              for (homotopy) pullbacks.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The homotopy pullback constructed in the way of Corollary
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomotopyPullbackByFactorizationLemma"}
              is an example of a
              = succeed "," do
                %em strict homotopy limit
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"}> homotopy limit
                \.
              In such a case, one can say that an arbitrary homotopy-commutative square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi W
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    W &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; Y
                    \\\
                    \\big\downarrow &amp;&amp; \big\downarrow
                    \\\
                    X &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; Z
                    }
            %p
              is a homotopy pullback square if the induced morphism from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              to the strict homotopy pullback is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
          %p A useful class of examples of this is implied by the following:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be a category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+presheaves"} simplicial presheaves
              over some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
              equipped with a
              %em local
              injective
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
              with respect to that site.
            %p
              Then an ordinary pullback of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mo ←
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to C \leftarrow B
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is a homotopy pullback already when one of the two morphisms is an objectwise
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The
              %em global
              projective
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+proper+model+category"} right proper
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomotopyPullbackByOrdinaryPullback"}
              the ordinary pullback in question presents the homotopy pullback in the global structure. By the discussion at
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield+localization+of+model+categories"} Bousfield localization of model categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-pullback"} (∞,1)-pullback
              of the diagram of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-presheaves"} (∞,1)-presheaves
              %em local
              model structure presents the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheafification"} (∞,1)-sheafification
              of this
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-presheaf"} (∞,1)-presheaf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact+functor"} left exact
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
              and hence preserves finite
